WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:02.267
-What's up, Jimmy?

00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:03.701
-It's good to see you, bud. It's always great to see you.

00:00:03.701 --> 00:00:05.234
How you doing? How's the family doing?

00:00:05.234 --> 00:00:06.801
How are the kids? -Everybody's good. Thank you.

00:00:06.801 --> 00:00:07.901
Yeah, my kids are good. -Yeah.

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:09.634
How old are the kids now?

00:00:09.634 --> 00:00:14.801
-My youngest, Marli, is 3. And Kensli is 6.

00:00:14.801 --> 00:00:18.133
And they're both about to celebrate birthdays, so add one.

00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:20.400
-You've been coming on the show for many years.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.601
-Yeah.

00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:23.334
-And you're usually wearing a hat.

00:00:23.334 --> 00:00:24.934
-Typically, yeah. -Yeah.

00:00:24.934 --> 00:00:27.267
With the number 3 on it. -This one. Exactly.

00:00:27.267 --> 00:00:29.133
-Yeah, I think the first time you came on,

00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:31.300
either you gave it to me or I took it.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.901
And then I had to give it back to you.

00:00:33.901 --> 00:00:36.501
[ Laughter ] -I do remember this.

00:00:36.501 --> 00:00:38.667
No, because I introduced the hat on this show. You're so right.

00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:40.100
-You only had one hat.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.767
-That was the fir-- That was literally the prototype.

00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:43.667
And I let you wear it. And then I was like, "Yo."

00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:45.801
-"I need it back. Sorry." [ Laughter ]

00:00:45.801 --> 00:00:47.501
-That was so embarrassing to ask for it back.

00:00:47.501 --> 00:00:48.868
-That was years ago, yeah. -Yeah.

00:00:48.868 --> 00:00:50.234
-It's been ten years, by the way.

00:00:50.234 --> 00:00:52.367
Ten years ago -- your first mixtape.

00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:53.667
-My first project, yeah. "10 Day."

00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:54.901
-Yeah. "10 Day" came out.

00:00:54.901 --> 00:00:57.501
What do you remember about putting that out?

00:00:57.501 --> 00:00:59.701
-Um... -Where were you?

00:00:59.701 --> 00:01:02.334
-I was -- I was in Chicago.

00:01:02.334 --> 00:01:05.467
And I remember I was a year out of high school.

00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:07.901
What I really remember was I was --

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:10.467
I had been, like, touring.

00:01:10.467 --> 00:01:13.100
I started, like, opening up for Childish Gambino

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.901
because I had shown him my mixtape.

00:01:14.901 --> 00:01:18.234
And I did a few dates as, like, an unknown opener.

00:01:18.234 --> 00:01:20.834
And like three weeks later, my mixtape came out.

00:01:20.834 --> 00:01:24.200
And it kind of just changed the scope of everything

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.000
'cause then people knew what they were coming to see.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.767
And they were like -- They'd be a few more fans.

00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:29.734
So it just gave me an opportunity to, like --

00:01:29.734 --> 00:01:31.601
I don't know -- to give stuff to people.

00:01:31.601 --> 00:01:33.868
'Cause I've always been a performer...

00:01:33.868 --> 00:01:35.067
-Yeah. -...a song maker.

00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:36.434
-Yeah. -But once you record it,

00:01:36.434 --> 00:01:38.100
once you make it, once you have a project,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.200
it just feels different.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.667
-You're always doing something new,

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.901
and every time you come on, I'm so happy and honored that

00:01:42.901 --> 00:01:45.234
you come on our show to announce stuff and talk about stuff.

00:01:45.234 --> 00:01:47.968
But you're doing this new festival in Ghana...

00:01:47.968 --> 00:01:49.400
-Yes. --...next year.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.400
It's called the Black Star Line Festival.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.934
-Yeah. -Talk to me about that.

00:01:53.934 --> 00:01:56.701
-Yeah, I'm very, very excited. Yeah.

00:01:56.701 --> 00:02:01.467
January 6th, we are going to West Africa,

00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:04.167
to Accra, Ghana,

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:09.334
and putting on a very, like, important event and --

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:12.634
-What inspired you to do it? -That's funny that you ask.

00:02:12.634 --> 00:02:16.000
I would love to talk a little bit about it, yeah.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.067
Well, in the 1920s --

00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:23.334
And actually in 1922, the U.S. government shut down

00:02:23.334 --> 00:02:26.634
a very, very important vessel

00:02:26.634 --> 00:02:28.367
and means of connection for Black people.

00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:30.501
There was this thing called the Black Star Line.

00:02:30.501 --> 00:02:35.167
It was created by Marcus Garvey and invested in by just,

00:02:35.167 --> 00:02:38.033
you know, common Black people around on the East Coast.

00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:42.300
And basically they had created a connection via trade

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.267
with this giant fleet of ships that he owned.

00:02:45.267 --> 00:02:48.567
So he had these ocean liners that would go between the U.S.

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:52.167
and all the Black islands and the continent,

00:02:52.167 --> 00:02:55.434
and it created, like, a network or connection

00:02:55.434 --> 00:02:57.133
for Black people globally.

00:02:57.133 --> 00:03:00.868
And he had a lot of, like, ideas about Black interconnectedness.

00:03:00.868 --> 00:03:03.467
And so I'm inspired.

00:03:03.467 --> 00:03:06.067
And there's a lot of people that are inspired by his works,

00:03:06.067 --> 00:03:09.367
his ideas, and also just the idea of global Blackness

00:03:09.367 --> 00:03:10.868
and being in connection.

00:03:10.868 --> 00:03:13.634
And so we're giving a -- we're giving it an opportunity.

00:03:13.634 --> 00:03:16.300
I'm very excited about it. -That's January 6th.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:18.133 --> 00:03:21.167
It's a lot to -- It's a giant thing to put on, a festival.

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:24.267
-Yeah. It's something I'm very, very excited about.

00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:25.667
-Good for you. -As you can tell.

00:03:25.667 --> 00:03:27.100
-Yeah, no, I like it.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.300
-But January 6th, like you said, the Black Star Line Festival.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:33.067
We invite Black people from everywhere, from --

00:03:33.067 --> 00:03:34.334
not just from the U.S.

00:03:34.334 --> 00:03:36.000
but from the U.K. and from the islands.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.868
And, you know, we're everywhere.

00:03:37.868 --> 00:03:41.901
So we invite you to come to Accra to be in a space of peace,

00:03:41.901 --> 00:03:44.834
of connectivity, of music, of art

00:03:44.834 --> 00:03:46.834
and just -- and just love.

00:03:46.834 --> 00:03:50.067
And I implore you to look up the Black Star Line and to --

00:03:50.067 --> 00:03:52.367
and to just, you know, get in tune.

00:03:52.367 --> 00:03:53.601
-Yeah.

00:03:53.601 --> 00:03:55.701
[ Cheers and applause ] That's why I love you.

00:03:55.701 --> 00:03:57.567
You're always doing something like that.

00:03:57.567 --> 00:03:58.701
You're always giving back

00:03:58.701 --> 00:04:00.868
and always making people think, as well.

00:04:00.868 --> 00:04:03.801
I love this. This is the -- "The Highs &amp; The Lows."

00:04:03.801 --> 00:04:06.234
-Yes. -Now, I like this because

00:04:06.234 --> 00:04:08.000
now you're -- you're doing a thing now

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.300
with every song that comes out, every project that comes out,

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:13.234
you put a different piece of artwork with it.

00:04:13.234 --> 00:04:15.534
-Yeah, yeah. So, this is the --

00:04:15.534 --> 00:04:20.200
This is the cover art, I guess, for "The Highs &amp; The Lows."

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:23.267
But it's also a photograph by Yannis Davy Guibinga,

00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:28.234
who is a photographer from Gabon, West Africa.

00:04:28.234 --> 00:04:31.501
And I met him in my travels as I've been doing this art stuff

00:04:31.501 --> 00:04:34.067
and kind of, like, collaborating with more artists.

00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:37.701
But, yeah, I've been trying to figure out ways to --

00:04:37.701 --> 00:04:43.634
to make my music translate in more ways, you know what I mean?

00:04:43.634 --> 00:04:46.067
I feel like I talk about sometimes things

00:04:46.067 --> 00:04:48.133
that are really heavy or very detailed.

00:04:48.133 --> 00:04:49.234
-Yeah.

00:04:49.234 --> 00:04:50.801
-And some people are visual learners.

00:04:50.801 --> 00:04:52.567
And so I've been getting way more into film

00:04:52.567 --> 00:04:54.467
and into, you know, the --

00:04:54.467 --> 00:04:57.400
the visual arts and working with artists like Yannis

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:58.901
or when I did -- -Where do you meet

00:04:58.901 --> 00:05:00.601
these artists? They come to you?

00:05:00.601 --> 00:05:03.234
-I -- I meet a lot of them through social media.

00:05:03.234 --> 00:05:05.267
So, luckily, we live in the future,

00:05:05.267 --> 00:05:06.567
like "The Jetsons."

00:05:06.567 --> 00:05:08.200
And we can just -- You can just meet people online.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:10.701
But I met -- The first artist that I did this with,

00:05:10.701 --> 00:05:15.200
Naila Opiangah, I met in Ghana in January.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.734
And she's a painter and architect

00:05:18.734 --> 00:05:21.968
that has lived all over and had a lot of different experiences.

00:05:21.968 --> 00:05:25.234
And me and her had some conversations about our art,

00:05:25.234 --> 00:05:26.801
how it can be commodified,

00:05:26.801 --> 00:05:29.767
how important it can be as a tool or a weapon.

00:05:29.767 --> 00:05:31.868
And it kind of rolled out into this thing where now

00:05:31.868 --> 00:05:35.367
every time I make a song, you know, I'm going to make sure

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:38.100
that people can receive it in as many ways as possible.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.701
And making something that's sustainable like,

00:05:40.701 --> 00:05:42.434
you know, a living piece of art

00:05:42.434 --> 00:05:43.701
is the type of stuff that I'm into.

00:05:43.701 --> 00:05:45.601
-Yeah. Isn't that kind of -- That's awesome.

00:05:45.601 --> 00:05:48.434
Because it's kind of -- I love that, because it's almost

00:05:48.434 --> 00:05:50.567
like what you were saying when you first started out

00:05:50.567 --> 00:05:52.434
and you were opening up for Childish Gambino.

00:05:52.434 --> 00:05:54.067
That's a big gig for you, and you're like,

00:05:54.067 --> 00:05:56.467
"But I got to give out --" You got to give out your mixtape.

00:05:56.467 --> 00:05:57.801
-Physical, yeah.

00:05:57.801 --> 00:05:59.300
-And you go, "That's what I'm doing."

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:00.934
And you're still doing that in a different way.

00:06:00.934 --> 00:06:02.234
Isn't that cool? -Thanks, man.

00:06:02.234 --> 00:06:04.200
-Come on, man. We love you, man. -I love you, dude!

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.100
You're just a great friend. [ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:08.133
-Chance the Rapper, performing later with Joey Bada$$.

00:06:08.133 --> 00:06:10.000
You want to stick around and play a game with me?

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.868
-I would love to play a game. -Let's play a game.

00:06:11.868 --> 00:06:13.367
Come on back. Chance and I are playing

00:06:13.367 --> 00:06:15.467
"Hey, Robot" when we come back. Come on, bud.

